From 17fd892b69825804f26454b81bfc55c3053044d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BSDKubernetes <bsdk8s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:34:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Enforce OCI linux.resources with rctl(8) rules on the
 container jail

FreeBSD has no cgroups, but CRI-O (and other engines) already express
container limits as OCI linux.resources. Translate them into rctl rules
on the container's jail at create time and remove them on delete (rctl
rules are keyed by jail name and outlive the jail):

  memory.limit         -> memoryuse:sigkill=<bytes>
  cpu.quota/cpu.period -> pcpu:deny=ceil(100*quota/period)
  pids.limit           -> maxproc:deny=<n>
  cpu.shares           -> ignored with a warning (no equivalent)

memoryuse:sigkill kills on RSS above the limit, the closest match to
memory.max + OOM kill. swapuse:deny was rejected: it charges writable
anonymous mappings at mmap time, so Go programs die at startup with
'out of memory allocating heap arena map' under a 64Mi limit.
memoryuse:deny only triggers reclaim and is a no-op without swap.

If RACCT is disabled (kern.racct.enable=0) the container still starts and
a warning is logged. Includes a dependency-free unit test (rctl_test).
